it's been a month since germany's  general elections though the country  still doesn't have a new government it  does have a new parliament and today the  new parliament younger more female and  more ethnically diverse met in session  for the first time  germany's social democrats are now the  biggest party outgoing a chancellor  angela merkel will be formally released  from office though she will remain as  caretaker chancellor until a new  government is in place  let's have a look at how the new  parliament shapes up with  736 members the bundestag is now one of  the largest parliaments in the world and  it has never been this big more female  law makers were elected this time than  last time but women are still a long way  from reaching parity in numbers  more than a third of the new intake are  women including the first two trans  women to take seats  more than 11 percent of the incoming  lawmakers have migrant backgrounds three  percentage points up on last time  dw political correspondent nina hazard  joins us from the bundestag  welcome nina what has been happening in  this first session and what can we  expect  well today was obviously the beginning  of a new era in german politics with the  new bundestag holding its very first  session that is always exciting but uh  today it was particularly exciting  because it was also the beginning of the  end of the era of anglais merkel the  chancellor she sat on the viewers ranks  today she was here at the session but  because she did she decided to not a run  to not run again during this election  she had to take a back seat essentially  so the bundestag has now elected its new  president and all the deputies and  interestingly it went for the third  woman in post-war german history babel  buzz from the social democrats so the  role of the president always goes to the  party with the biggest group in the  parliament and that is this time around  the social democrats and later today  angela merkel and her ministers will  receive their formal documents releasing  them officially from their duties but  that government will stay in power as  caretaker government until the next  government is sworn in  so it's not just angela merkel uh  leaving though is it it's her party  leaving a leaving government after 16  years  and that's going to be a big change  isn't it  that is going to be a big change and  here to talk about exactly that what  does that mean for the conservatives to  go into opposition i have somebody from  the opposition from the cdu from the  conservatives thomas heinemann so how  are the cdu how are you going to shape  that role of opposition party you're not  used to it we're not used to it and we  don't like to use it to be used to it  now but we have to accept it  quite frankly we don't know how it will  go and  we have to learn it  you are a  digital expert angela merkel in 1999  declared you the honorary internet  expert of the cdu  and now 22 years later  germany is lagging behind when it comes  to digital infrastructure when it comes  to all things internet where did your  party go wrong  i mean  the the problem is that we have to  change the culture we have to change the  culture in the entire country so we i  talk about cities across our states and  we've got nationwide and and this  council change didn't take place  honestly and we needed to have corona to  be aware of it  um  and we now made big change changes um  but they don't you can't really see it  now but it will have a big change within  the next year so  this will go with a new government  yeah exactly that new government those  parties at least they have declared  digital all things digital one of their  top priorities along with a fight  against the climate crisis so what is  your role then why do we need the  conservatives what are the new  conservatives going to be which topics  will you  try and represent  some colleagues and myself we made an  initiative a year and a half ago called  neustar where we declare it has to be  the number one priority and what we have  to gonna do and we're pretty happy that  the new government said we want to  continue this project  and our role is known to control that  they really do so  okay well good luck with that good luck  with getting used to that new role of  opposition leader  thank you very much tomozam and you have  to run off to another meeting so we'll  let you go thank you very much for being  with us thank you  all right that was thomas heimann one of  the digital experts with the cdu the  conservatives and glomerulus  conservatives who are having to get used  to that new role of opposition party and  so with new parties trying to form a  government nina what sort of uh policies  can we expect from this parliament  well we've briefly touched upon it  already of course the social democrats  are now the biggest party group in the  new bundestag and  they have over taken over from the  conservatives they are currently in  negotiations in coalition talks with the  greens and the fdp  to form that new government that  hopefully will have by christmas and of  course they have a big majority here if  that  government actually comes together  between the social democrats the greens  and the ftp but they are not natural  allies so there are quite a few sticking  points still in those coalition talks  and they will go on for a couple of  weeks still but they all say that  they're optimistic that they'll overcome  the biggest hurdles and that we will  hopefully have a new chancellor a lead  government led by chancellor olaf  schultz by mid december all right thank  you for that dw political correspondent  nina haaser  so plenty of young first-time mps in  germany's new parliament dw uh  correspondent thomas sparrow met two of  them to hear about their hopes and  expectations  two fresh faces for the new german  parliament  and two very different political  realities  it's florian muller's first day as an mp  the 34 year old represents the center  right cdu party  does holding guns was on the attack  today is a very special day for me i  have been elected to the bundestag for  the first time gruelling an intense  campaign is behind me and now it's a  matter of actually becoming a proper mp  muller may be excited but his party is  in crisis  it had its worst ever result in  september's election and it's now  preparing for life in opposition after  16 years of leading the country with  angela merkel  that's our job not to be fundamentally  in opposition but to take a constructive  approach to ensure that this country is  well governed  riya schroeder's liberal fdp party knows  life in a position only too well  the 29 year old first-time mp is excited  this is too young it's of course  exciting to go into the plenary hall for  the first time now as a member of  parliament but i also feel a great  responsibility and i'm also honored to  be here  after four years in opposition the  liberals are currently in coalition  talks to join the next government  many young people voted for them and  that's particularly important for  schroder  i want to work to ensure that the  perspective of young people has an  impact here that the issues that are  important to young people education  climate protection digital development  are also on the agenda  muller  two new mps that stand for the profound  political change that is just beginning  to unfold as germany enters the post  merkel era

